{
"segments": [
{
"text": "Case Seven.",
"start": 0.75,
"end": 1.753,
"pause": 0.6
},
{
"text": "The Dog That Stayed Quiet.",
"start": 2.3529999999999998,
"end": 3.974,
"pause": 1.4
},
{
"text": "In the front garden of Loveday Nance's cottage stood Sir Reginald, the finest garden gnome in Tidewhistle Cove. He had a red hat, a white beard and a fishing rod, and he had won the gnome prize at the summer show three years running.",
"start": 5.374,
"end": 19.753,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "On Tuesday morning, Sir Reginald was gone.",
"start": 20.603,
"end": 23.291,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Loveday's garden is surrounded by a high, thick hedge, and the only way in is through the little wooden gate. Every night her terrier, Pickle, sleeps in the porch facing that gate. Pickle barks at everyone who comes through it. He barks at the postman, who has delivered letters for five years. He barks at the vicar. He even barked at Agnes Bell last week, though she brought him a biscuit. The only two people Pickle never barks at are Loveday herself and her grandson Pip, who takes Pickle for a walk every single day.",
"start": 24.141,
"end": 56.135,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Loveday had gone to bed at ten, and Sir Reginald had been in his usual place then. Her neighbour, old Mr Hocking, had sat up reading by his open window until two in the morning. He said the night was perfectly still, and that Pickle had not barked once.",
"start": 56.985,
"end": 73.07,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Agnes Bell listened to all this over a cup of tea in Loveday's kitchen.",
"start": 73.92,
"end": 78.336,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "\"I have three ideas,\" said Loveday, counting on her fingers. \"That holidaymaker, Mrs Ashby, who kept saying she would give anything for a gnome like him. Mr Fenwick from the grocer's, whose own gnome has lost to Sir Reginald three years in a row. And, well, Pip was round yesterday, but he's a good boy.\"",
"start": 79.186,
"end": 98.407,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "Pickle, in his basket, thumped his tail.",
"start": 99.257,
"end": 101.775,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Can you solve it? Who took Sir Reginald the gnome?",
"start": 102.875,
"end": 105.605,
"pause": 1.2
},
{
"text": "Think about it.",
"start": 106.805,
"end": 107.829,
"pause": 0.9
},
{
"text": "Take a moment, or pause here if you are listening. When you are ready, the solution follows.",
"start": 108.729,
"end": 113.956,
"pause": 4.5
},
{
"text": "The Solution.",
"start": 118.456,
"end": 119.48,
"pause": 1.1
},
{
"text": "Pip Carew took the gnome.",
"start": 120.58,
"end": 122.052,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "The garden can only be entered through the gate, and Pickle sleeps facing that gate and barks at everybody, even the postman and the vicar. Mr Hocking was awake by an open window until two in the morning and never heard a single bark. So whoever came through the gate was someone Pickle knew so well that he didn't bark at all.",
"start": 122.902,
"end": 142.87,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "There are only two such people: Loveday herself, and Pip. Mrs Ashby and Mr Fenwick would have set Pickle off at once.",
"start": 143.72,
"end": 151.037,
"pause": 0.85
},
{
"text": "When Agnes asked him gently, Pip confessed straight away. Sir Reginald's red hat had faded in the sun, and Loveday's birthday was on Friday. Pip had taken the gnome to his shed to repaint him as a surprise. On Friday morning, Sir Reginald was back by the gate with a hat as bright as a postbox, and Loveday cried happy tears.",
"start": 151.887,
"end": 172.687,
"pause": 0.0
}
],
